```c
#include <stdio.h>

int count_digits(long num) {
    int count = 0;
    if (num == 0) return 1;
    if (num < 0) num = -num;
    while (num > 0) {
        num /= 10;
        count++;
    }
    return count;
}

int main() {
    long number;
    printf("Enter a number: ");
    if (scanf("%ld", &number) != 1) {
        printf("Invalid input.\n");
        return 1;
    }
    printf("Number of digits: %d\n", count_digits(number));
    return 0;
}
```

The provided code is already well-formed and the issues mentioned in the comments (test cases failing with no output) seem to stem from an external testing context, not from the code shown. The code logically handles counting digits for positive, negative, and zero values, and it checks for valid input in `main`. Therefore, no changes are needed for the given source code.